ActualOutput_Q3:
Achieved
Monthly Progress Reports during the quarter under review have been  submitted to  DWYPD (October, November and December 2022) on the progress made against the targets set for the quarter; 
During the quarter, various awareness sessions (virtual, and in-person/contact) on the GBV&F Implementation Plan, SOGIE and the Code of Good Practice on the elimination of Harassment in the Workplace have been facilitated; 
Workplace Harassment Awareness Sessions were rolled out in the following offices:
13 October 2022 	-	eMalahleni, Mpumalanga Province;
19 October 2022 	-	Ga-Rankuwa, Gauteng Province; 
28 October 2022 	-	Makhado, Limpopo Province;
9 November 2022 	- 	Mahikeng, North West Province;
17 November 2022	- 	King Williams Town, Eastern Cape Province;
18 November 2022	-	Dundee, KwaZulu-Natal Province;
19 November 2022 	-	Drakensberg, KwaZulu-Natal Province;
22 November 2022 	-	George, Western Cape Province; and 
6 December 2022 	-  	Welkom, Free State Province
Marketing materials to raise awareness of GBV&F in the form of posters have been procured and distributed to offices,  (HQ& Provinces);
16 Days of Activism For No-Violence Against Women and Children was commemorated on 6 December 2022 in Springbok Office to remember the life of Ms Carmen Rossouw, (DHA official stationed at Springbok Office, Northern Cape Province) was a victim of GBV.  She passed away in 2021 during the 16 Days of Activism For No-Violence Against Women and Children; 
The Ikhaya Magazine published during the quarter under review, carried two articles related to (i) Disability Rights Awareness month and (ii) 16 Days Of Activism for No-Violence against Women and Children Campaign; 
First Provincial Women’s Forum was launched in KwaZulu-Natal on the 19 – 20 November 2022.

ReasonforDeviation_Q3:
Limited LR resources throughout the Department. 

Referral of cases for Formal Disciplinary action, whereas these matters should be dealt with as Progressive Discipline; resulting in an unnecessary increase in the workload of the Directorate: Labour Relations. 

Operational challenges - 
1) Unreasonably long postponements of cases requested / sick leave of officials; 
2) Dependency on witnesses who are public members, and don't want to avail themselves for cases due to intimidation; 
3) Load shedding as some matters are conducted virtual, whereas in-person hearings are costly; 
4) New charges added to existing charge sheet;
5) Presiding Officers not complying with turn-around-times in submitting verdicts / sanctions; 
4) Complexity of cases; and 
5) Budget.
